12837	{"artifactId":"LLD-315d510cb28100e2-s9","entry":{"id":"review-dec6ba845072f561a64550952c152898","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-23T06:34:01.307Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every acceptance criterion ac1-ac8 of the EXT for s9 is designed: the hook is widened (ac1-ac3), the terminal approve is reused (ac4), refresh on git_pull/git_push plus an explicit refresh (ac5, ac6), a derived artifactId/revision in the shown record (ac7), and a port-based status read (ac8).","evidence":"EXT.md lists ac1..ac8. The LLD has contracts 2.1-2.6 and data changes 3.1-3.4 covering each one, and its §6 acceptance mapping has one row per ac. The k4 conflict that ac2 creates is acknowledged as a decision and an open question, and EXT ac2 itself calls it 'a deliberate change from today'. [files: docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/S009/EXT.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The explicit refresh in ac5 ('the user can ask for the same refresh explicitly') has a designed surface.","evidence":"syncTracker already backs an explicit refresh: src/cli/services/workflow.ts:411 `return syncTracker(repoPath, epicHash);` and the tracker.sync orchestrator path (src/workflow/orchestrator.ts:2933). The LLD says the same function backs the explicit refresh, and the new deps parameter is optional, so these callers keep compiling. [files: src/cli/services/workflow.ts, src/workflow/orchestrator.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Today ensureDraftIssues returns not-applicable/no-team-policy for a repo with no policy or with teamMode false, and makes no tracker call in that case.","evidence":"src/workflow/draft-issues.ts:110 `if (policy.value === 'none' || !policy.value.teamMode) return { status: 'not-applicable', reason: 'no-team-policy' };`. This return comes before loadTracker at line 125. [files: src/workflow/draft-issues.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Today syncTracker reads issue state directly through gh (resolveGithubConfig + ghAuthOk + ghGetIssueState) rather than through the port, and writes epicStatus/storyStatus/lastSyncedAt via patchTrackerMeta.","evidence":"src/workflow/tracker/sync.ts:38-60. It calls resolveGithubConfig, ghAuthOk, ghGetIssueState and mapIssueStatus, then `patchTrackerMeta(defineJson, { epicStatus, storyStatus, lastSyncedAt })`. [files: src/workflow/tracker/sync.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_pull and git_push return a failed ToolResult on a git error and success:true otherwise, so a refresh after success can be added as a warning without changing failure handling.","evidence":"pull.ts:90-104 and push.ts:116-135 return fail(...) when code is non-zero, and otherwise return `{ output, format:'markdown', success:true, data }`. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/pull.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/push.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The GitHub adapter implements readIssueStatus by reusing ghGetIssueState, and the result carries the port failure kinds (missing tool, not signed in, unreachable after the 10-second timeout, rejected).","evidence":"src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:317-318: ghGetIssueState calls `out('gh', [...])`. `out` (line 35-37) is `_exec(cmd, args, { encoding: 'utf8' })`, with no timeout and no classifyGhFailure. Only runGh (lines 86-97) applies GH_TIMEOUT_MS and throws a classified GhCommandError. Wrapped as written, a slow gh would block git_pull/git_push with no time limit (this breaks ac6 and invariant c4), and every failure would map to 'rejected'. [files: src/workflow/tracker/github.ts, src/workflow/tracker/github-adapter.ts]","action":"Implement readIssueStatus with runGh(['issue','view',n,'--repo',...,'--json','state,labels']), or move ghGetIssueState onto runGh, so the 10-second timeout and the failure classification apply. Add contract tests for a timeout and for the missing-tool and missing-signin kinds.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The terminal approve (ac4) reuses the same unit-issue ensure code as the draft hook, so it creates only missing issues.","evidence":"src/workflow/tracker-auto.ts:240, 277, 281, 323, 326 and 331 call ensureUnitIssueDetailed. draft-issues.ts:131 calls the same function from ./tracker/unit-issue.js. [files: src/workflow/tracker-auto.ts, src/workflow/draft-issues.ts, src/workflow/tracker/unit-issue.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Adding 'status-read' to Capability needs changes only at port.ts:21 (the union) and github-adapter.ts:57 (the declared set); loadTracker will then reject a tracker that lacks it.","evidence":"src/workflow/tracker/port.ts:127 `const ALL_CAPABILITIES: readonly Capability[] = ['claim', 'issue-create', 'signoff-request', 'completion'];` and line 131 `return ALL_CAPABILITIES.filter(c => needed.has(c) && !declared.has(c));`. A capability missing from ALL_CAPABILITIES is never reported as missing. Because the array is not checked for exhaustiveness, the compiler would not flag the omission, and loadTracker (github-adapter.ts:318) would accept a tracker without status-read, so the k8 error path would never fire. [files: src/workflow/tracker/port.ts, src/workflow/tracker/github-adapter.ts]","action":"Add port.ts:127 (ALL_CAPABILITIES) to the call sites in §3.1, and add a unit test that missingCapabilities reports 'status-read'.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The IssueReadiness change (a new no-tracker reason, and solo repos now getting ready/not-ready) is handled at every consumer.","evidence":"The only consumers are readinessFields (draft-issues.ts:148-151), called at src/daemon/steps/workflow-step/phases/synthesize.ts:92 and src/daemon/workflow-rpc.ts:349. readinessFields maps any not-applicable to {}, so a new reason value needs no change at the call sites. Solo-with-tracker repos will now show notices, which is the intent of ac2. [files: src/workflow/draft-issues.ts, src/daemon/steps/workflow-step/phases/synthesize.ts, src/daemon/workflow-rpc.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.3","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"git_push's postcondition 'after a successful push, refreshTrackerStatus runs' covers git_push's own dryRun input.","evidence":"src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/push.ts:41 declares `dryRun` and line 110 passes `--dry-run`; a dry run still returns success:true (line 135, with pushed:false). The LLD excludes dry runs from the refresh only for git_pull (edge case 'git_pull with dryRun true'). As written, a dry-run push would trigger a tracker refresh although nothing was pushed. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/push.ts]","action":"State in §2.6 and the edge cases that the refresh runs only when data.pushed is true (no refresh on a dry-run push), and add an integration test for it.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"No stored field is added to TrackerMeta; artifactId and revision are derived on display, and the refresh writes only the existing epicStatus/storyStatus/lastSyncedAt fields, so older records stay readable.","evidence":"src/workflow/tracker/refs.ts:51-60: TrackerMeta already has epicStatus, storyStatus and lastSyncedAt. patchTrackerMeta (lines 111-121) merges through carryTracker and writes 'meta-only'. With no new stored keys, no migration is needed, and a missing meta.revision reads as 0 under the sc3 rule. [files: src/workflow/tracker/refs.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"s9 does not redesign sc1 (owned by s1). It adds only an optional readIssueStatus method and a capability value, proposed as an amendment, and leaves the existing port members alone.","evidence":"port.ts:90-117 already uses optional members for later additions (for example `readSignoffRequest?` was added by s8). The LLD's §4 marks sc1 as 'consumes' with a methodAdd amendment, leaving s1 as the owner. The s1 adjacent-scope text explicitly defers 'an issue-status read on the port' to the define extend, which is s9. [files: src/workflow/tracker/port.ts, docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/HLD.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"refreshTrackerStatus enforces a total time bound, so 'a slow tracker ends in a failed result' within 'one overall time bound' across several Epics.","evidence":"refreshTrackerStatus is specified as synchronous (it returns TrackerRefreshReport, not a Promise). The gh wrappers run synchronously through _exec (github.ts:86-97), so an overall deadline can only be checked between calls; it cannot interrupt one. The worst case is (Epics × (1 + Stories) + whoAmI) × 10 s from loadTracker (github-adapter.ts:322) and the per-issue reads. The LLD does not say how the bound is enforced or what its value is. [files: src/workflow/tracker/github.ts, src/workflow/tracker/github-adapter.ts]","action":"Specify the bound: a deadline checked before each gh call, with Epics or Stories left unread reported as failed or skipped 'time limit reached'. Give its value. Add a unit test with a fake port that is slow on every read.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A policy-less repo with no tracker configured is detected without any tracker call (ac3), and is told apart from a misconfigured tracker.","evidence":"loadTracker (github-adapter.ts:314-325) resolves the target and then calls whoAmI (a gh call). The LLD gives DraftHookDeps 'a reader of the tracker target', presumably resolveStrictTrackerTarget (config/github.ts:349-373). That function returns the same strictRefuse shape for 'not configured / type none' as for invalid JSON or a bad entry. The LLD does not say whether a malformed github.json in a solo repo becomes not-applicable or not-ready. [files: src/workflow/config/github.ts, src/workflow/tracker/github-adapter.ts]","action":"State which target reader decides 'no tracker' and how a malformed config is reported (not-ready with the reason is recommended). Add a unit case for it.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each acceptance criterion ac1-ac8 maps to at least one named test at a runnable level (unit, contract or integration), with smoke only as an extra.","evidence":"§6 maps ac1-ac8 to unit, contract or integration tests. ac2 and ac5 add smoke tests on top of unit and integration tests, and ac6 and ac8 each have a unit or contract test as well as integration. Fixtures include a fake TrackerPort, the gh exec seam (_exec in github.ts) and a temp git repo with a bare remote. [files: src/workflow/tracker/github.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":2,"med":2,"low":11,"unverified":1},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-23T06:34:01.307Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
